/* Where his projected 1500 would have finished in May.

   Task: he wants to run track as well as cross country, so a 1500 field is a real
   test rather than a single team-best mark. For every school on the board this
   holds three things:

     d15  the team's own 2026 outdoor 1500 depth — the fastest mark by each
          distinct athlete, best first, up to seven of them (nath is how many
          different men ran the event at all). dslot is where his projected 1500
          of 4:01.0 would sit in that list, so dslot 3 means two of their men were
          faster all season.
     cm   their conference outdoor championship: every man who ran the 1500 at
          that meet (n), the fastest time run there (win), and where 4:01.0 would
          have placed among all of them (place). The final is reported separately —
          fn finishers, fwin winner, flast the slowest qualifier, so flast is the
          bar for making the final, and fplace is where he would have landed in it.
          theirs is this team's entrants at that meet, best mark each, times only;
          nfinal is how many of them made the final; slot is his place among them.
          An empty theirs means the team entered nobody in the 1500 — which is
          itself an answer about whether they race the event.
     post  the same shape for any postseason meet where this team had a 1500
          runner: an NCAA first round or national championship, or the IC4A-ECAC.

   Times are seconds, as written on TFRRS to the hundredth. Athletes are not named
   here for the same reason they are not named in XCRACES — the times are what the
   comparison needs. Every meet carries its TFRRS results URL so any row can be
   checked at the source. Read with methodology.html §7.

   Tactical finals are real: a conference final can be slower than its own prelims,
   which is why `place` (everyone at the meet, on time) is the honest headline and
   `fplace` is shown next to the winner rather than alone.

   The decathlon's 1500 is excluded. TFRRS files it as a second "1500 Meters" event
   page at the same meet, and counting it inflated field sizes and dropped
   multi-eventers into teams' entrant lists, which is why n, theirs and nath here
   are open-1500 only. The test for which page is which is the meet's own: a meet
   page links every event the meet actually held, and the decathlon's internal 1500
   is not one of them — the decathlon is listed as the decathlon. That is exact,
   where the shape of the race (no scoring column, a small field, a winner 40 to 90
   seconds down) is only usually right: an invitational that scores nothing has an
   unscored open 1500 too. */
